WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
- When I came out of the closet as gay,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.560
there was almost no backlash.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.020
Some people were kind of surprised.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.480
I got probably what you got,

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.430
which is a lot of people said,
well, you don't seem gay,

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.770
because they want you to have taffeta

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.450
and sparkles and something,

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.820
but I didn't get a lot of anger, really.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.880
But coming out politically,
coming out politically,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.860
suddenly I had friends turning on me.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.550
I didn't have any
friends that turned on me

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.423
about being gay.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.230
If anything, it
strengthened my friendships

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.520
because we were being more honest,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.770
or at least I was being
more honest with them.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.180
But when I came out politically,

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.740
just saying I'm frustrated with the left,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.290
it wasn't that I had
said I'm a conservative

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.200
or whatever you want to call it,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.320
but suddenly I was getting a ton of hate.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.340
I mean I'm talking about people

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.210
that were invited to my wedding,

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.500
people that I had been
lifelong friends with saying

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.837
all sorts of terrible things,
and that is kind of weird.

00:00:49.837 --> 00:00:52.754
(mysterious music)

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.900
- Hello, I'm Douglas Murray,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.773
and welcome to the "Don't
Burn This Book" book club.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.760
I am here today talking
with Dave about chapter one

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.760
of "Don't Burn This Book",
which is on coming out.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.430
First of all, Dave, I should ask you,

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.814
well, first of all,
congratulations on the book.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:23.170
- Thanks.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.140
- Second, there are two comings out

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.650
you mention in this chapter.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.470
Maybe we could talk a
little about the first,

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.050
because I was very struck by it.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:36.000
Every gay man has a coming out story,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.440
every gay woman has a coming out story.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.850
But it's a reminder of
how tortuous, difficult,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.030
and in some cases, delayed,

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.950
the process of discovering
who you are can actually be.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.401
- It's interesting because
it's one of those things,

00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:53.150
like you, I talk for a living.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.570
Everyone knows what I
think about everything,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.880
but it is a part of my life
that I don't talk about

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.490
that often, and I liken being
closeted about my sexuality

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.900
to what I think many,
many people right now

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.100
are suffering from,
which is being closeted

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.010
about their political views,
and I don't meant that

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.240
in the context of some
really racist political view

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.900
or even fringe political view.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.620
I mean people that have
views that, 10 years ago,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.230
were very moderate and decent,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.950
now are just simply afraid
to say what they think.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.380
In terms of my sexuality in coming out,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.430
it's a true story that I mention in there

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.320
that the first person
that I ever came out to

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.610
was a friend of mine who
was a stand up comic,

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:32.940
he was an openly gay comic.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.648
I hate the phrase openly gay,
but he was an openly gay comic

00:02:35.648 --> 00:02:37.480
named Mike Singer in New York City,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.640
and I was 25 years old, so that's late.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.400
Especially for young
people now, when they hear

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.770
oh, you didn't come out till
25, that sounds crazy to them

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.860
because they're out at 12,
or at least talking about it

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.670
as if it's not a bad
thing, something like that.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.470
The first person I came out to,

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.470
it was September 11th,
2001, at about 12:30 a.m.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.420
in the Times Square subway station,

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.040
so almost everyone that's watching this

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.320
can probably picture that.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.010
It's the most famous subway
station, probably, in the world.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.890
We were right at the shuttle train.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.840
All the shuttle does
is go from Times Square

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.550
to Grand Central, back and forth,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.670
so you're in this odd
sort of purgatory place.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.190
I blurted this thing out, what I thought

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.320
was this horrific secret
that I couldn't hide anymore,

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.910
and then literally about
seven or eight hours later,

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.200
America was under attack.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.240
I mean I woke up from a
phone call from my dad

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.230
who could see the first
tower had been hit.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.362
He worked in New York City, he
could see it from his office.

00:03:35.362 --> 00:03:38.100
I kid you not, I genuinely thought

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:43.040
it had something to do with
me releasing this evil secret

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.100
into the world, and the way I liken that

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.000
to the political part
is that it's really hard

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.470
to live one life just as a human being,

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.940
just to be who you are and
put your life out there

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.190
and find some purpose and all that,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.290
that's very difficult in and of itself.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.220
But if you're closeted about something,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.340
in this case your sexuality,
or your political thoughts,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.160
you will lie without even
realizing you're lying,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.130
you will live more than one life.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:08.740
You will have two sets of friends,

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.570
you will have family members
that don't know who you are,

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:12.540
all of these things.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.360
I think people really
do understand this now

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.940
from the political side, because
I hear this all the time.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.550
Someone will come up to me
at a college gig or something

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.130
and say, do you know
what, I'm a libertarian,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.856
something like that,
or even the scary one,

00:04:23.856 --> 00:04:25.780
I'm a secret conservative.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.360
Or they'll say I'm a
closeted conservative,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.300
and it's usually a
young person at college.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.140
It's like if you start that
path now when you're young

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.100
not saying what you
think, it will break you

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.350
at some point down the road.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.250
Yeah, I think a lot of gay people

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.763
just have an extra understanding of that.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.940
- By the way, also that story you tell,

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:51.940
it's a horrifying story about
connecting these two events,

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:56.307
your personal life and then
this terrible catastrophe.

00:04:56.307 --> 00:04:59.200
It's also sort of a
reminder to me, at any rate,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.600
of the way in which when we
store things up like that,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.897
we create these sort of
catastrophic ideas in our head

00:05:07.897 --> 00:05:10.980
of what admitting what we
are, whether it's sexuality

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.350
or your opinions, your political
views, what that can do.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.130
It means you've run through
all of these catastrophes

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.070
in your brain, and then
one has actually happened

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.370
in the real world, so of course
you've connected the two.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.930
This is a horrifying reminder
of what this is like.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.325
- It's interesting,
because it sounds crazy

00:05:29.325 --> 00:05:31.480
to think that I woke up the next morning.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.610
That sound legitimately crazy,

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.660
but it also tells you a lot
about what the closet is like.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.300
I always say that in the closet,
there's only room for one.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.120
When you are solo, whatever it is

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.960
that you're not telling
people that you think,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.790
it doesn't mean you're right
about everything, by the way.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.800
You may have all the wrong
beliefs that you're hiding,

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.710
whatever that is, but when
you are not telling people

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:54.110
who you are, not sharing what you are,

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.610
the only way we can figure
out what's real in life

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.670
is if we can map that
against other people.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.290
when you say something to other people,

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:01.910
you express yourself in some way

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.152
and then they can say something back.

00:06:04.152 --> 00:06:06.640
Look, you, Douglas, as a writer,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.620
you put you thoughts out there,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.610
and then you're gonna get a
certain amount of pushback

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.460
on some things, and then you might go,

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.540
oh, well maybe I wasn't
thinking about this

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.970
the right way, and then
you can actually change

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.883
the way you think, you can
look at things in a new way.

00:06:19.883 --> 00:06:22.160
When you're alone and
solitary and doing that,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.020
it affects every part of
you, which by the way,

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.620
I don't go too far into this.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.940
There's only one chapter, actually,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.180
that we cut in the entire
book, is when I went

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.500
really personal on some
of that struggle stuff,

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.160
and we sort of felt it was going

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.470
into another book altogether.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.930
It will lead you down a really dark path

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.107
that I think most gay people know about.

00:06:42.107 --> 00:06:46.010
If you're not yourself,
if you are someone else,

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.790
whoever that other person is,

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.190
you will do all sorts of
things that are not good

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.563
for the person that you truly are.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.703
- That brings us to the other coming out

00:06:57.703 --> 00:07:01.070
in this first chapter, and the light

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.400
that your first coming
out had on the second one.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.170
The second coming out, of
course, is your coming out as,

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.003
well, what would you call it?

00:07:08.003 --> 00:07:10.227
No longer of the left
or something like that.

00:07:10.227 --> 00:07:11.090
What would you call it?

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:14.103
- At least at that time
that's what I thought it was.

00:07:14.103 --> 00:07:18.110
You were one of the first
guests on my show at that point,

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.860
so this is now the fall of 2015,

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.900
and I finally started
saying what I thought,

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.820
and I was doing it from
a lefty perspective.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.790
I was saying, hey, guys, it's our side

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.530
that's having the problem here.

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.310
Everyone can say the
conservatives are nuts

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:32.150
and whatever you want to say about them,

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.810
but I'm seeing a problem here.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.180
We're the ones silencing people,

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.750
we're the ones calling everyone racist,

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.940
we're the ones not tolerating dissent

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.740
or open inquiry or any of these things.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.300
Then suddenly I started by saying it.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.180
This is the sort of metaphor to it.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.410
It's like I started saying that,

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.370
and then suddenly I had Sam
Harris on the first show.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.690
Then from that, I had Maajid Nawaz on,

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.970
who was coming from a similar perspective,

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.080
despite a very, very different
history that Maajid had.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.580
Then I talked to you, I
talked to Ayaan Hirsi Ali,

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.430
I talked to Christina Hoff Sommers.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.430
It goes back to what I said

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.040
about mirroring my reality
against somebody else.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.650
Here were these people
that were extremely diverse

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.490
in where they were from
and their skin color

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.220
and sexuality and all of that stuff,

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.610
but they were feeling the same thing

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.629
about roughly what our side was.

00:08:18.629 --> 00:08:22.310
I think from that you
start getting empowered,

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.040
because you go, whoa, I'm not alone.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.230
There's other people who think like this.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:28.820
Again, that does go very much

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:30.970
to when you come out
of the closet sexually

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:32.520
and then you meet another gay person,

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.590
and you go, whoa, they're not evil.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:39.200
They're not this broken,
crushed, destroyed thing.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.740
Then it allows you not to
think that about yourself.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.820
I started coming out
politically in effect,

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.970
and what I didn't realize
was that there was gonna be

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.110
a whole hell of a lot of backlash to that.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:51.690
I thought people were gonna be like,

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.200
oh, yeah, let's do it, and by
the way, a lot of people did.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.340
I mean that's where it
all started bubbling up

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.140
and it sort of got me to where I am now.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.870
But I didn't realize that.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:04.030
It's like when I came
out of the closet as gay,

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:05.540
there was almost no backlash.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.990
Some people were kind of surprised.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.190
I got probably what you got,
which is a lot of people said,

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:12.100
well, you don't seem gay,
because they want you to have

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.430
taffeta and sparkles and something,

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.800
but I didn't get a lot of anger, really.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:20.860
But coming out politically,
coming out politically,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.820
suddenly I had friends turning on me.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:24.540
I didn't have any
friends that turned on me

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:25.714
about being gay.

00:09:25.714 --> 00:09:28.220
If anything, it
strengthened my friendships

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.480
because we were being more honest,

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.750
or at least I was being
more honest with them.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.160
But when I came out politically,

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:35.730
just saying I'm frustrated with the left,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.270
it wasn't that I had
said I'm a conservative

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.170
or whatever you want to call it.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.290
But suddenly I was getting a ton of hate.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.580
I mean I'm talking about people that were

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.810
invited to my wedding,
people that I had been

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:49.290
lifelong friends with saying
all sorts of terrible things.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.230
That is kind of weird,
when you just start saying,

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:55.373
hey, here I am, and then people
go, well now you're evil.

00:09:56.263 --> 00:09:58.323
- It's kind of connected, isn't it?

00:09:59.166 --> 00:10:01.680
I'm not surprised that you said

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.470
when you came out as gay you
didn't have much pushback.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.610
Of course there are people that do,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.930
growing up in other areas of the country

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:09.260
and other parts of the world, as we know,

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.670
but I'm not surprised that you didn't have

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.970
much pushback then, but
if you had gone back

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:17.310
a couple of decades earlier
or several decades earlier,

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.390
you would have had a very similar thing

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.100
to what you're experiencing politically.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.710
It seems to me that the common factor is

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:27.300
that in the not so distant past,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.460
one of the objections to
having gay people around

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.300
was the sort of idea
that basically gay people

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.910
made other people gay in some
way, particularly younger men,

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.610
that somehow the proximity to a gay

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.198
was somehow catching in some way.

00:10:40.198 --> 00:10:41.250
- That sounds right.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.850
- Yeah, I would that it were.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:48.850
But the political fact in
this is the same thing.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:54.420
It's this fear that these
people who are around

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:59.610
who have these ideas that
aren't absolutely in lock step

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:03.345
with a particular dogma of the day,

00:11:03.345 --> 00:11:05.509
other people are gonna catch it.

00:11:05.509 --> 00:11:10.380
If we're not careful, our kids
will grow up not far left,

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.540
and then where will we be?

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:15.760
There's something about the toxic idea.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.620
- Yeah, that's really interesting.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:22.170
I probably, and maybe it
will be for a next book

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.230
or an essay that you'll write,

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.570
that concept really should be
extrapolated a little bit more

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.150
because I think you're right.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.770
You come out of the closet and it's like

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.950
your friends don't
suddenly turn gay, right?

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.160
Everyone, if they're straight,

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:35.190
they're straight, and that's it.

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:36.730
I never met a friend that I came out to,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.040
and suddenly they were
like, well, I'm gay too,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.350
although I guess you could
have a closeted friend too

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:42.480
or something like that.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.290
You're not gonna convert someone.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.470
It's not a thing.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:50.110
But you are right that when you
start coming out politically

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:51.710
and you start talking to people

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:53.320
that know more things about you.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.250
I started talking Thomas
Sowell about economics.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.930
I started talking to
Larry Elder about racism.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:01.380
- These are dangerous people.

00:12:02.256 --> 00:12:03.570
These are dangerous people.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.820
- Highly dangerous people
with deeply contagious ideas.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:11.000
What happens is, in that case, yes,

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.580
and that must be the real threat,

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.410
that it's not just you that they're

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.450
trying to take out at that point.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.000
It's not just you that
they want to silence,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.240
it's that they might go, holy cow,

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.890
some of these ideas aren't that bad.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.560
But they have unfortunately,
and this was my frustration

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:26.800
with the left from the beginning,

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:30.110
they had become so
rigid in their thinking.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:31.140
Of course, and I always say this,

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.010
I don't mean every single leftist,

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:36.200
but I mean broadly lefties these days.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.980
It goes in cycles, right?

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.520
20 years ago it was John
McCain and Joe Lieberman

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.160
who were Republicans
that were trying to get

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.270
video game violence, they didn't want

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.380
mortal combat in video game stores.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.887
So these things are cyclical for sure,

00:12:48.887 --> 00:12:51.590
and it may eventually turn around again,

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:52.560
but I think you're right.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.860
The idea is you come out
politically and you start saying

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.590
just some of the stuff that I now say.

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.430
I talk about low taxes, I
talk about state's rights,

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.240
I talk about individual rights.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.940
Well, that stuff does spread
once people hear about it

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:09.020
because I think it's a much
clearer path to happiness

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:12.540
and a functional society,
and if you don't want that,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.610
well, what have you got
to do to those people?

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.550
I guess you've got to
treat them as apostates,

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:18.780
and in effect, that's
what's happened to me

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.083
and a lot of other people.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.200
- Deviants, that's what people are.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.046
- Political deviants.

00:13:26.046 --> 00:13:27.795
- Political deviants.

00:13:27.795 --> 00:13:30.060
It's a very interesting thing.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.946
It tells us a lot about a society.

00:13:31.946 --> 00:13:34.570
We know it's sort of
commonplace to say it,

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:37.280
but you learn a huge
amount, if not everything

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.760
about a society by finding out

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.250
what is effectively taboo
or what is sort of deemed

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:44.710
to be socially unacceptable.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:49.000
One of the oddities about
this journey you describe

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.687
of coming out as no longer on the left,

00:13:52.687 --> 00:13:54.460
one of the oddities of it is, of course,

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.283
is that loads of people
are not on the left.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.010
Governments keep on being
elected by majorities,

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:06.870
by populations who are not of the left.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.790
One of the things that it always throws up

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:14.190
is how does a political
side that in many ways

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:19.190
has kept on losing still
have this stranglehold

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:23.470
that means that people
are as fearful as ever,

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:27.260
perhaps more fearful, of
coming out as not being

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:31.090
of a particular part of the leftist tribe?

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:32.750
- Yeah, it's super interesting.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:34.440
I know you've talked
about this a bunch too,

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:37.130
and I go into it further
later in the book,

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.170
but I think mostly what
it is is that the left

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:43.780
really did the culture
game better than the right.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.490
If you're on the right, your basic idea

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.880
is you sort of want individual rights,

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.490
and then you don't really
want the government involved,

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.250
so you don't feel this need
to be part of everything

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:57.430
outside of your family or
your work or your community,

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.650
something like that.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.420
But on the left, it seems to me that

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:03.970
no longer on the left is
there a unifying principle.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:05.520
It's very obvious that on the right,

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.220
it doesn't mean they always
hold that ideal properly,

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.540
but the ideal is individual rights.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:11.760
If you're a member of a society,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:12.760
you should be treated equally.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.920
It doesn't mean we all have
the same starting point,

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.710
it doesn't mean some
of us don't work harder

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:19.240
or aren't luckier or a
series of other things,

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:20.390
but on the left, it seems to me

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.630
that there isn't a unifying principle

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:26.420
that brings them together, so
that leads to the hysteria.

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.810
It leads to a certain religious nature

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.630
of if you aren't exactly
what I am when I am.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.783
It's also that you have to
change the second they change.

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:38.890
If the second they're for gay marriage,

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:42.120
everyone better be for it that day.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:42.953
- Yes.

00:15:42.953 --> 00:15:46.920
- Barack Obama ran first time
around against gay marriage,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.430
and the progressives of
2040 will hate that man.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:54.960
They will say he was a bigot
because he didn't change,

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.400
and they'll also do it retroactively

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.780
when they weren't even alive
and have any understanding

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:01.120
of what it was like to be alive in 2012.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:04.570
But I think that thing
leads to a constant,

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.330
oh, I feel something about
something right this moment,

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.470
and that's good enough for
me to put forth to the world,

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.730
so if you don't agree
with it at that moment,

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.500
you're screwed or you're
evil or the rest of it.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:18.520
Then that leaves an
awful lot of good people

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.840
just going, all right, maybe
the right isn't that terrible.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:25.100
And generally what you
find, I mean you know this,

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:26.823
is that they're not that terrible.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.450
- One of the other
interesting things about that,

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:35.140
by the way, is this
thing of excommunication.

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.273
It strikes me as being very interesting.

00:16:38.273 --> 00:16:40.680
I mean there have been, as you know,

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:42.190
I wrote about this in my latest book,

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:44.400
in "The Madness of Crowds",
but there's this thing

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.800
of trying to excommunicate
people from the church of gay

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:51.290
or from being Black if they've
not got the right views

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.390
and so on, this madness
that has been going on

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.080
in recent years.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:59.140
It's interesting, this
excommunication trend,

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:02.198
also it exists, in my
experience, pretty much solely,

00:17:02.198 --> 00:17:06.640
in politics, on the
left, in that to be told

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:09.230
as you were told by people, you know,

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.510
you're not any longer of our tribe

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:14.460
because you've effectively apostasized.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.460
You've broken the rules, you're out.

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:19.710
One of the things that always strikes me

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.700
as being interesting about
this, I may be wrong,

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:23.980
there may be countries where this happens,

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:27.698
but I've never seen this
happen on the right.

00:17:27.698 --> 00:17:31.010
I don't know about you,
but if somebody said to me,

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:34.690
you know, Douglas, I don't think
you're a real conservative,

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.157
I would be like, so.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.200
- Yeah.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:41.400
- That's fine, you know.

00:17:42.413 --> 00:17:44.170
You're not really going
to be on the right.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:45.810
We are going to excommunicate you

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:49.100
from the right wing group.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.930
I'd be like, who are you

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:55.460
or what gave you these
magical wand waving powers?

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:58.500
But it does happen and it does work.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:01.560
This excommunication,
these magic spell words,

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.630
it works on the left.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:07.780
- I think I can give you probably
the most simplest example

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:08.770
of what you're talking about.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.800
Look, I make the case later in the book

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.870
for several things that
are thought of as lefty.

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:15.610
Let's just throw gay marriage aside.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:16.870
I happen to be married to a man.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:20.030
That's thought of as a
liberal or lefty thing,

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:21.100
so that's one.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.250
That's an easy one.

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:25.290
I make a pro choice argument in this book.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.025
That is gonna piss off
a lot of conservatives.

00:18:28.025 --> 00:18:30.670
For most conservatives, that is the rail

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:33.540
you do not touch, but I
do make that argument.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:35.160
I am against the death penalty.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:37.740
Most conservatives are
for the death penalty.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.750
I'm for some level of public education.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:42.060
I went to the State University
of New York at Binghamton.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.610
I believe the state does
have some role in education.

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.610
I'm for dignity with
death, that you should have

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:48.330
the choice at the end of your life.

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:51.570
I mean these are things that
are thought of as lefty,

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.840
and I have several others as well,

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.610
but I get no love from anyone
on the left about that.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:19:00.220
Meanwhile, I can sit down with
Ben Shapiro, that scary guy,

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:01.890
or Glenn Beck, that scary guy,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.030
or the scariest of all, Douglas Murray,

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:05.480
and we can sit down.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:06.880
- Yeah, I have a couple
issues about that with you,

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:08.772
by the way, that you've just listed.

00:19:08.772 --> 00:19:10.080
We'll get to that another time.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:11.750
- All right, we'll have
to arm wrestle about those

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:13.770
next time, but that's the point.

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:14.610
That's the point.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:18.510
Even if we had some truly intractable

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:22.210
political position that
we could not get over

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:27.180
after 10 drinks and
eight hours of doing it,

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:29.460
I know you wouldn't
excommunicate me at the end,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.270
and you know I wouldn't do it to you.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.800
If anything, I know this
Douglas, because we've done this.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:37.340
We've had great political
debates, not publicly,

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.440
where we don't fully get there equally,

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:41.820
and that's the joy in it.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:43.737
That's the joy of like, whoa, maybe,

00:19:43.737 --> 00:19:46.330
and I say this to Shapiro all
the time about gay marriage,

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:49.136
where his position is
a religious position.

00:19:49.136 --> 00:19:51.950
He's not trying to stop
my marriage anymore.

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:53.710
It's not that he fought for gay marriage,

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.370
and I'm very open about that,

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:58.920
but it's like I believe that
if Ben and I keep talking about

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:01.630
gay marriage, or not even
talking about gay marriage.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.460
If the future, if Ben and
I can just remain friendly

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:06.450
and have conversations about politics

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.770
for the next 50 years, I
suspect that when I'm 93

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:12.950
and Ben is 89, or whatever it is,

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.610
he might actually come around and be like,

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:17.720
I guess Rubin was kind of right on that.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.800
But there's only one way to find out.

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:22.780
So when you talk about
how one side purges you,

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:25.300
it's like how much penance
can I give these people,

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:27.240
if I agree with you on this,
I agree with you on this,

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.600
I agree with you on this, this, this,

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:30.850
and then have them just spit in my face,

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.640
while the other side, I
take some of the issues

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.940
that are most heartfelt to them.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.040
I mean the abortion one, that is the one.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:41.182
You know that, for people on the right.

00:20:41.182 --> 00:20:44.250
And yet I take the counter position,

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:46.730
and I know, I mean at the
end of the abortion part,

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:48.880
I say now that you all
hate me, let's move on,

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:53.230
but I know that while they
will disagree with me,

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:55.580
they will not try to destroy me,

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:58.440
and that is just the
fundamental difference.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.137
- Well, you know there are two
things I'll just add to that.

00:21:00.137 --> 00:21:02.860
The first is the importance of humility

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:05.430
in all arguments and discussions.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.380
Humility means that we
can keep our ears open

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.740
and keep our minds open.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:12.920
The second thing that
often follows on from that

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:16.130
is that there is very little in the world,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.060
certainly very little
in the world of ideas,

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.130
that is more stimulating than the moment

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:22.180
when you realize that you might be wrong.

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:26.370
Keeping yourself open to that possibility

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:28.125
seems to be worth doing.

00:21:28.125 --> 00:21:31.150
I'm conscious that our time's probably up,

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:32.782
but we could go on all day.

00:21:32.782 --> 00:21:33.810
- We could.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.910
- This has been a
terrifically enjoyable book.

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:38.351
It gets off to a racing start.

00:21:38.351 --> 00:21:39.810
- Thank you, thank you.

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.750
From you, that is a true compliment.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.610
I do have to tell you something publicly

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:46.660
that I've said to you
privately many times.

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:49.440
As I was writing the book, I
had just read your last book,

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:53.480
and I read pretty much
anything you put out there.

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.120
The entire time, as I
was writing sentences,

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.380
I always had this thing
in the back of my head,

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:00.260
would Douglas Murray write this better?

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:02.210
Would Douglas Murray write this better?

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:06.905
I think I did a pretty
decent job for round one,

00:22:06.905 --> 00:22:10.040
but let's just keep
fighting the fight, man,

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.117
and I appreciate you doing
this, so thanks a lot.

00:22:13.117 --> 00:22:13.950
- It's a great pleasure.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:15.560
See you, Dave.

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:18.477
(mysterious music)

